var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/#User-Guide-and-Verification-Workflows","page":"User Guide","text":"","title":"User Guide & Verification Workflows"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#1.-Design-by-Contract-(DbC)","page":"User Guide","text":"JuLean provides a flexible contract specification system supporting two complementary authoring modes:\n\nNon-Invasive Docstring Contracts (Recommended for Libraries): Specify contracts directly inside standard Julia Markdown docstrings using - \\@requires ...`,- `@ensures ...`, and- `@invariant ...`. This allows your library to maintain zero dependencies onJuLean.jl` while exposing human-readable specifications in documentation.\nIn-Body Macro Contracts (For Verification Scripts & Kernels): Annotate functions with @formalize or @contract and place @requires, @ensures, and @invariant directly inside the function body.","title":"1. Design-by-Contract (DbC)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Core-Contract-Clauses-and-Syntax","page":"User Guide","text":"@requires condition [message]: Input preconditions verified prior to body execution.\n@ensures condition [message]: Postconditions capturing the returned value in variable result.\n@invariant condition [message]: Invariants enforced on each iteration of loops and block states.\nImplication operators: implies(p, q), p ⟹ q, p --> q.","title":"Core Contract Clauses & Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Mode-A:-Non-Invasive-Docstring-Contracts","page":"User Guide","text":"In libraries that prioritize zero external dependencies (such as Sirocco.jl), contracts live directly in the Markdown docstring:\n\n\"\"\"\n    clamp_flow(f, lb, ub)\n\nClamps numerical flow `f` within bounds `[lb, ub]`.\n\n- `@requires lb <= ub`\n- `@ensures lb <= result <= ub`\n\"\"\"\nfunction clamp_flow(f, lb, ub)\n    return f < lb ? lb : (f > ub ? ub : f)\nend\n\nJuLean extracts the AST and docstring contracts non-invasively:\n\nusing JuLean\n\n# Reflect AST and parse contracts from Markdown docstring:\nJuLean.formalize!(clamp_flow)\n\n# Verify with Z3 SMT solver:\nres = JuLean.verify(:clamp_flow; backend=:Z3)\n\n# Or wrap dynamically for runtime checking in test environments:\nsafe_clamp = JuLean.contracted(clamp_flow)","title":"Mode A: Non-Invasive Docstring Contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Mode-B:-In-Body-Macro-Contracts","page":"User Guide","text":"For dedicated formalization modules or interactive experimentation:\n\nusing JuLean\n\n@formalize function clamp_flow(f::Real, lb::Real, ub::Real)::Real\n    @requires lb <= ub\n    @ensures lb <= result <= ub\n    if f < lb\n        return lb\n    elseif f > ub\n        return ub\n    else\n        return f\n    end\nend","title":"Mode B: In-Body Macro Contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Global-Toggle-for-Runtime-Contracts","page":"User Guide","text":"For zero production overhead, disable runtime assertions:\n\ndisable_contracts!()\n# Critical high-throughput loops run with zero assertion overhead\nenable_contracts!()\n\n","title":"Global Toggle for Runtime Contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#2.-Mathematical-Quantifiers","page":"User Guide","text":"Use @forall and @exists in contract expressions:\n\n@formalize function find_min(x::Real, y::Real)::Real\n    @requires x >= 0 && y >= 0\n    @ensures @forall(t::Real, (t <= x && t <= y) ⟹ t <= result)\n    return x < y ? x : y\nend\n\nIn Julia: Evaluated as all(...) / any(...) dynamically.\nIn Lean 4: Transpiled to ∀ (t : ℚ), ...\nIn SMT-LIB: Transpiled to (forall ((t Real)) ...)\n\n","title":"2. Mathematical Quantifiers"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#3.-Composite-Structures-and-Extensible-Types","page":"User Guide","text":"Annotate structs with @formalize struct to generate Lean 4 structures and SMT datatypes:\n\n@formalize struct Interval\n    lo::Real\n    hi::Real\nend\n\n@formalize function span(iv::Interval)::Real\n    @requires iv.lo <= iv.hi\n    @ensures result >= 0\n    return iv.hi - iv.lo\nend","title":"3. Composite Structures & Extensible Types"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Extensible-Type-Mapping","page":"User Guide","text":"Client packages (like Sirocco.jl) can register domain-specific mappings:\n\nregister_lean_type!(GMCFProblem, \"DunesLean.GMCFProblem\")\n\n","title":"Extensible Type Mapping"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#4.-Bounded-Model-Checking-(BMC)-for-Loops","page":"User Guide","text":"For iterative algorithms, unroll loops to depth k:\n\n@formalize function loop_sum(n::Int)::Int\n    @requires n >= 0\n    @ensures result >= 0\n    s = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < n\n        @invariant s >= 0 && i >= 0\n        s = s + i\n        i = i + 1\n    end\n    return s\nend\n\nres = JuLean.verify(:loop_sum; backend=:BMC, depth=5)\n\nIf an iteration breaks the invariant, Z3 extracts the exact variable states at that step.\n\n","title":"4. Bounded Model Checking (BMC) for Loops"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#5.-Unsat-Core-Minimal-Precondition-Extraction","page":"User Guide","text":"Z3 automatically isolates the minimal set of preconditions needed for verification:\n\nres = JuLean.verify(:strict_positive; backend=:Z3)\nprintln(res.unsat_core) # e.g. [\"pre_1\"]\n\n","title":"5. Unsat-Core Minimal Precondition Extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#6.-Exporting-Certified-Artifacts","page":"User Guide","text":"Export all verified structures and specifications into a standalone, compilable Lean 4 source file:\n\nJuLean.export_lean_file(\"build/VerifiedModule.lean\")\n\n","title":"6. Exporting Certified Artifacts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#7.-Non-Intrusive-Contracts-via-Docstring-Reflection","page":"User Guide","text":"For high-performance packages (e.g. Sirocco.jl), you often want zero dependencies on JuLean.jl in your production codebase. JuLean provides a docstring reflection engine that reads contracts directly from standard Markdown docstrings:\n\n\"\"\"\n    reduced_cost(cost, pi_u, pi_v, gamma)\n\nCompute the generalized reduced cost of an arc.\n\n- `@requires gamma > 0`\n- `@ensures result == cost - pi_u + gamma * pi_v`\n\"\"\"\nfunction reduced_cost(cost, pi_u, pi_v, gamma)\n    return cost - pi_u + gamma * pi_v\nend\n\nIn your package extension (loaded conditionally via [weakdeps]):\n\nusing JuLean\nusing MyPackage\n\n# Reflect method AST and parse docstrings automatically:\nJuLean.formalize!(MyPackage.reduced_cost)\n\n# Verify with Z3 or Lean 4:\nres = JuLean.verify(:reduced_cost; backend=:Z3)\n\n","title":"7. Non-Intrusive Contracts via Docstring Reflection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#8.-Runtime-Instrumentation-and-Assertion-Stripping","page":"User Guide","text":"Production kernels frequently contain internal contract checks or debug invariants (e.g., _check_dual_equations!). When transpiled to SMT or Lean 4, imperative error-throwing logic would fail to translate.\n\nJuLean allows you to mark debug checks as runtime-only:\n\n# Register debug guards and assertion functions\nJuLean.register_runtime_only!(MyPackage._contracts_enabled)\nJuLean.register_runtime_only!(MyPackage._check_bounds!)\n\n# Statements calling these functions are automatically stripped from the formal AST\nJuLean.formalize!(MyPackage.my_kernel)\n\nFurthermore, JuLean automatically unwraps performance pragmas:\n\n@inbounds, @simd, @inline, @noinline, @fastmath, @propagate_inbounds\n\nThis guarantees that performance optimizations in Julia do not interfere with formal transpilation.\n\n","title":"8. Runtime Instrumentation & Assertion Stripping"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#9.-Function-Inlining-in-Formal-Specifications","page":"User Guide","text":"When a formalized kernel calls auxiliary helper functions, JuLean can inline them automatically during SMT-LIB2 and Lean 4 generation:\n\n# Define and register an inlinable helper:\n@inline_formal function abs_diff(x::Real, y::Real)::Real\n    return x >= y ? x - y : y - x\nend\n\n# Or register an existing function manually:\nJuLean.register_inline!(:safe_div, [:num, :den], :(den != 0 ? num / den : 0))\n\nAny call to abs_diff or safe_div inside a @formalize kernel is expanded into the kernel's expression tree prior to verification.\n\n","title":"9. Function Inlining in Formal Specifications"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#10.-Imperative-Code-Transpilation-and-Lean-4-Id.run-do","page":"User Guide","text":"JuLean bridges imperative Julia algorithms to purely functional Lean 4 code using Lean 4's Id.run do monadic block:\n\nIn-Place Array Mutation: arr[idx] = val in Julia transpires to arr := arr.set! (idx - 1) val in Lean 4.\nRange Iteration: for i in 1:n in Julia transpires to for i in [:n] do in Lean 4, with automatic 1-based to 0-based index translation.\nWhile Loops: while count > 0 transpires to while count > 0 do with mutable state variables (let mut).\nCompound Assignments: += and -= are translated into assignment rebindings.\n\n@formalize function countdown(n::Int)::Int\n    @requires n >= 0\n    @ensures result == 0\n    count = n\n    while count > 0\n        count -= 1\n    end\n    return count\nend\n\nTranspiles to Lean 4:\n\ndef countdown (n : Int) : Int :=\n  Id.run do\n    let mut count := n\n    while count > 0 do\n      count := count - 1\n    return count\n\n","title":"10. Imperative Code Transpilation & Lean 4 Id.run do"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#11.-Equality-Saturation-and-Metatheory.jl-Integration","page":"User Guide","text":"JuLean optionally integrates with Metatheory.jl via the JuLeanMetatheoryExt package extension. When Metatheory.jl is loaded in your environment:\n\nusing Metatheory\nusing JuLean\n\n# Algebraic expressions are simplified using E-graph term rewriting:\nexpr = :(x + 0 * y + (z - z))\nsimplified = JuLean.simplify_expr(expr)\n# => :x","title":"11. Equality Saturation & Metatheory.jl Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Contracts,-Runtime-Enforcement-and-Docstring-Reflection","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Contracts, Runtime Enforcement & Docstring Reflection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Function-Inlining-and-Runtime-Instrumentation-Stripping","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Function Inlining & Runtime Instrumentation Stripping"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Specifications,-Verification-and-Domain-Types","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Specifications, Verification & Domain Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Lean-4-Transpiler,-Monadic-Blocks-and-AST-Translation","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Lean 4 Transpiler, Monadic Blocks & AST Translation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#SMT-LIB2-Generation,-Z3-and-Bounded-Model-Checking","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"SMT-LIB2 Generation, Z3 & Bounded Model Checking"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Toolchain-Configuration-and-Lean-4-Runner","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Toolchain Configuration & Lean 4 Runner"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Code-Export-and-Term-Rewriting","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Code Export & Term Rewriting"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@contract","page":"API Reference","text":"@contract function ... end\n\nEnforces contracts (@requires, @ensures) on a function at runtime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@contract"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@formalize","page":"API Reference","text":"@formalize function ... end\n@formalize struct ... end\n@formalize Module.func\n\nAnnotates a Julia function or struct definition with formal contracts:\n\nFor struct: extracts the LeanStructure, registers it in STRUCT_REGISTRY & CUSTOM_TYPE_REGISTRY.\nFor function: extracts FormalSpecification, instruments runtime contracts, and enables formal verification.\nFor function reference (Module.func or func): automatically extracts contracts from the function's Markdown docstrings!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@formalize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.formalize!","page":"API Reference","text":"formalize!(func::Function, argtypes::Tuple=()) -> FormalSpecification\n\nFormalizes a function directly by extracting its implementation and docstring contracts, and registers it in SPEC_REGISTRY and INLINE_REGISTRY.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.formalize!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.contracted","page":"API Reference","text":"contracted(func::Function, argtypes::Tuple=()) -> Function\n\nWraps an existing Julia function and returns a new function that dynamically checks preconditions (@requires) and postconditions (@ensures) parsed from func's Markdown docstring whenever JuLean.CONTRACTS_ENABLED[] is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.contracted"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@requires","page":"API Reference","text":"@requires condition [message]\n\nDeclare a function precondition.\n\nCan be used in two complementary ways:\n\nInside Markdown docstrings (recommended for zero-dependency libraries): Annotate standard Julia docstrings with -@requires <condition>`. JuLean extracts these non-invasively via docstring reflection (JuLean.formalize!(func)`), allowing client libraries to declare formal contracts without taking a dependency on JuLean.\nInside function bodies: Placed directly in functions annotated with @contract or @formalize (or used standalone for runtime precondition checking).\n\nThrows ContractViolationError if condition evaluates to false when contracts are enabled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@requires"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@ensures","page":"API Reference","text":"@ensures condition [message]\n\nDeclare a function postcondition.\n\nCan be used in two complementary ways:\n\nInside Markdown docstrings (recommended for zero-dependency libraries): Annotate standard Julia docstrings with -@ensures <condition>`. Inside postconditions,resultrefers to the return value of the function. Extracted non-invasively by JuLean's reflection engine (JuLean.formalize!(func)`).\nInside function bodies: Placed directly in functions annotated with @contract or @formalize. Inside postconditions, result refers to the return value of the function.\n\nThrows ContractViolationError if condition evaluates to false upon function return.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@ensures"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@invariant","page":"API Reference","text":"@invariant condition [message]\n\nDeclare a loop or state invariant.\n\nCan be used in two complementary ways:\n\nInside Markdown docstrings: Annotate standard Julia docstrings with -@invariant <condition>`` for non-invasive contract specification extracted during reflection.\nInside loop bodies: Placed directly inside while or for loops in functions annotated with @contract or @formalize. Enforced on every iteration at runtime, and verified symbolically via Bounded Model Checking (JuLean.verify(fn; backend=:BMC)).\n\nThrows ContractViolationError if condition evaluates to false during loop execution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@invariant"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.requires","page":"API Reference","text":"requires(condition::Bool, msg::AbstractString=\"\")\n\nRuntime check for a precondition. Throws ContractViolationError if condition is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.requires"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.ensures","page":"API Reference","text":"ensures(condition::Bool, msg::AbstractString=\"\")\n\nRuntime check for a postcondition. Throws ContractViolationError if condition is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.ensures"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.invariant","page":"API Reference","text":"invariant(condition::Bool, msg::AbstractString=\"\")\n\nRuntime check for an invariant. Throws ContractViolationError if condition is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.invariant"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@forall","page":"API Reference","text":"@forall(var in collection, condition)\n@forall(var::Type, condition)\n\nUniversal quantifier specification.\n\nFor discrete collections or ranges (e.g. i in 1:n), evaluates all(i -> condition, collection) at runtime.\nFor typed domains (e.g. x::Real), evaluated symbolically by Lean 4 and SMT solvers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@forall"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@exists","page":"API Reference","text":"@exists(var in collection, condition)\n@exists(var::Type, condition)\n\nExistential quantifier specification.\n\nFor discrete collections or ranges (e.g. i in 1:n), evaluates any(i -> condition, collection) at runtime.\nFor typed domains (e.g. x::Real), evaluated symbolically by Lean 4 and SMT solvers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@exists"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.implies","page":"API Reference","text":"implies(p::Bool, q::Bool) -> Bool\np ⟹ q -> Bool\np --> q -> Bool\n\nLogical implication operator: p ==> q is true unless p is true and q is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.implies"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.enable_contracts!","page":"API Reference","text":"enable_contracts!(enabled::Bool=true)\n\nEnable or disable runtime contract checking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.enable_contracts!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.disable_contracts!","page":"API Reference","text":"disable_contracts!()\n\nDisable runtime contract checking (for zero-overhead production execution).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.disable_contracts!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.CONTRACTS_ENABLED","page":"API Reference","text":"Global toggle for dynamic contract checking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.CONTRACTS_ENABLED"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.ContractViolationError","page":"API Reference","text":"ContractViolationError\n\nException thrown when a precondition, postcondition, or invariant fails at runtime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.ContractViolationError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.extract_contracts","page":"API Reference","text":"extract_contracts(body::Expr) -> (preconditions, postconditions, invariants, clean_body)\n\nInspects a function body AST, extracts all @requires, @ensures, @invariant calls, and returns the separated conditions and the remaining executable body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.extract_contracts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.parse_doc_contracts","page":"API Reference","text":"parse_doc_contracts(doc::AbstractString) -> Tuple{Vector{Expr}, Vector{Expr}, Vector{Expr}}\n\nExtracts formal contracts from a Markdown docstring:\n\nLines matching @requires <expr>\nLines matching @ensures <expr>\nLines matching @invariant <expr>\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.parse_doc_contracts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.get_method_docstring","page":"API Reference","text":"get_method_docstring(m::Method) -> String\n\nRetrieves the raw docstring associated with method m by querying Julia's doc metadata or reading the source comments immediately preceding the method definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.get_method_docstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.auto_extract_method_ast","page":"API Reference","text":"auto_extract_method_ast(func::Function, nargs::Int) -> Union{Tuple{Vector{Symbol}, Expr}, Nothing}\n\nInspects func methods with arity nargs, uses InteractiveUtils.functionloc to locate the source file and line, parses the function AST from the original source file, and returns (param_names, clean_body_expr).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.auto_extract_method_ast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.instrument_contract_body","page":"API Reference","text":"instrument_contract_body(fn_name::Symbol, pre::Vector{Expr}, post::Vector{Expr}, clean_body::Expr)\n\nConstructs the instrumented Julia AST that checks preconditions before running the body, captures the return value in result, checks postconditions, and returns result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.instrument_contract_body"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#JuLean.@inline_formal","page":"API Reference","text":"@inline_formal function foo(args...) ... end\n\nDefines a Julia function and registers its AST with JuLean so that any call to foo(...) inside a @formalize kernel is automatically inlined during SMT and Lean 4 transpilation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.@inline_formal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.register_inline!","page":"API Reference","text":"register_inline!(fn_name, args::Vector{Symbol}, body)\nregister_inline!(fn_name, fdef::Expr)\n\nRegisters a function or method AST to be automatically inlined inside formal specifications.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.register_inline!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.expand_inlines","page":"API Reference","text":"expand_inlines(ex) -> Expr\n\nRecursively expands calls to functions registered in INLINE_REGISTRY (or dynamically inspected from enclosing Julia packages) by substituting actual arguments into the registered parameter template.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.expand_inlines"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.INLINE_REGISTRY","page":"API Reference","text":"INLINE_REGISTRY\n\nGlobal registry mapping function identifiers (Symbol or Expr) to inlining templates (args::Vector{Symbol}, body::Expr). Functions registered here are transparently inlined by JuLean's SMT and Lean 4 engines whenever called within @formalize specifications.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.INLINE_REGISTRY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.register_runtime_only!","page":"API Reference","text":"register_runtime_only!(f::Function) -> f\nregister_runtime_only!(name::Symbol) -> name\n\nRegisters f (or name) as a runtime-only helper. Any statement of a formalized function body that contains a call to a runtime-only name is dropped from the transpiled body — whole-statement, never partially rewritten. Typical use: a solver package keeps debug contract checks inside its kernels behind a predicate such as if _contracts_enabled() ... end and registers the predicate (and the check helpers) here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.register_runtime_only!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.strip_runtime_only","page":"API Reference","text":"strip_runtime_only(ex) -> Expr\n\nRemoves every statement containing a call to a register_runtime_only!-ed name, recursively through nested blocks (if / for / let bodies). Returns ex unchanged when nothing matches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.strip_runtime_only"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.RUNTIME_ONLY_REGISTRY","page":"API Reference","text":"RUNTIME_ONLY_REGISTRY\n\nGlobal registry of function names (Symbol) whose call sites are runtime-only instrumentation: debug contract checks, assertions, or logging attached to the running implementation. Statements in @formalized bodies that call a runtime-only function are stripped before SMT / Lean 4 transpilation (see strip_runtime_only), so such instrumentation can live inside the real kernel without breaking the formal pipeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.RUNTIME_ONLY_REGISTRY"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.FormalSpecification","page":"API Reference","text":"FormalSpecification\n\nHolds the extracted contract specification and metadata of an annotated Julia function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.FormalSpecification"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.PosInt","page":"API Reference","text":"PosInt\nNodeId\nArcId\nPNat\n\nType aliases representing strictly positive integers (1, 2, 3, ...), transpiled to Lean 4 as ℕ+ (PNat). Indices of this type can be converted to 0-based Lean array indices via p.1 - 1 without any toNat cast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.PosInt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.VerificationStatus","page":"API Reference","text":"VerificationStatus\n\nStatus of a formal verification attempt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.VerificationStatus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.VerificationResult","page":"API Reference","text":"VerificationResult\n\nOutcome of verifying a specification using Lean 4, SMT/Z3, Metatheory, or Runtime testing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.VerificationResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.is_verified","page":"API Reference","text":"is_verified(res::VerificationResult) -> Bool\n\nReturns true if the verification attempt succeeded, i.e. the specification was proven valid (Lean 4) or no counterexample was found (Z3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.is_verified"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.verify","page":"API Reference","text":"verify(spec::FormalSpecification; backend=:Z3, timeout=30.0, tactic=\"\") -> VerificationResult\n\nVerifies a FormalSpecification using the designated backend:\n\n:Z3: Rapid automated falsification and counterexample check via Z3 SMT solver.\n:Lean4: Mechanized proof verification using Lean 4 and Mathlib.\n\n\n\n\n\nverify(func::Function, argtypes::Tuple=(); kwargs...) -> VerificationResult\n\nVerifies a Julia function directly by extracting its implementation and docstring contracts.\n\n\n\n\n\nverify(fn_name::Symbol; backend=:Z3, kwargs...) -> VerificationResult\n\nVerifies a registered function previously annotated with @formalize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.verify"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.verify_with_z3","page":"API Reference","text":"verify_with_z3(spec::FormalSpecification; logic::String=\"\", timeout::Float64=30.0) -> VerificationResult\n\nVerifies a specification using Z3.\n\nunsat: The specification holds for all inputs satisfying preconditions -> StatusVerified.\nsat: A counterexample violating postconditions was found -> StatusCounterexample.\nunknown: SMT solver was inconclusive -> StatusUnverified.\n\nSoundness: specifications without postconditions (vacuous falsification target) and specifications with imperative loops (dropped by the SMT translation) are rejected as StatusUnverified rather than reported as verified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.verify_with_z3"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.verify_bmc","page":"API Reference","text":"verify_bmc(spec::FormalSpecification; depth::Int=3, logic::String=\"ALL\", timeout::Float64=30.0) -> VerificationResult\n\nPerforms Bounded Model Checking (BMC) by unrolling loop iterations in SMT. The falsification target is not (invariants ∧ postconditions) at the final unrolled state; unsat means the loop kernel satisfies its specification for every execution of exactly depth iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.verify_bmc"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.SPEC_REGISTRY","page":"API Reference","text":"SPEC_REGISTRY\n\nGlobal registry mapping function names (Symbol) to their extracted FormalSpecification. Every function annotated with @formalize registers its specification here, making it available to verify, export_lean_file, and verification_summary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.SPEC_REGISTRY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.extract_formal_spec","page":"API Reference","text":"extract_formal_spec(func::Function, argtypes::Tuple=()) -> FormalSpecification\n\nExtracts a formal specification directly from a Julia function:\n\nBody is parsed from source code using Base.functionloc.\nContracts (@requires, @ensures) are extracted from the function's docstring.\n\n\n\n\n\nextract_formal_spec(fdef::Expr) -> FormalSpecification\n\nAnalyzes a Julia function definition expression, parses arguments, type annotations, return type, docstring, and extracts all @requires and @ensures contracts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.extract_formal_spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.extract_struct_decl","page":"API Reference","text":"extract_struct_decl(sdef::Expr) -> LeanStructure\n\nExtracts a LeanStructure declaration from a Julia struct definition AST.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.extract_struct_decl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LeanType","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract base type for Lean 4 types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LeanType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LeanExpr","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract base type for Lean 4 expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LeanExpr"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LeanDecl","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract base type for Lean 4 declarations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LeanDecl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LeanDoStmt","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract base type for Lean 4 imperative statements inside a do block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LeanDoStmt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.to_lean","page":"API Reference","text":"to_lean(t::LeanType) -> String\n\nRenders a LeanType into Lean 4 type syntax.\n\n\n\n\n\nto_lean(e::LeanExpr; precedence=0) -> String\n\nRenders a LeanExpr into Lean 4 expression syntax with appropriate parenthesization.\n\n\n\n\n\nto_lean(b::LeanDoBlock; indent::Int=2) -> String\n\nRenders a sequence of imperative do statements ending with a return value.\n\n\n\n\n\nto_lean(decl::LeanDecl) -> String\n\nRenders a LeanDecl into Lean 4 declaration syntax.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.to_lean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.to_lean_stmt","page":"API Reference","text":"to_lean_stmt(stmt::LeanDoStmt; indent::Int=2) -> String\n\nRenders an imperative Lean 4 do statement with proper indentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.to_lean_stmt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.transpile_to_lean_decl","page":"API Reference","text":"transpile_to_lean_decl(spec::FormalSpecification; tactic=\"\") -> (LeanDef, Union{LeanTheorem, Nothing})\n\nGenerates the Lean 4 def and, when postconditions are declared, the verification theorem. A specification without postconditions has nothing to prove, so no theorem is emitted (nothing is returned in its place).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.transpile_to_lean_decl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.synthesize_tactic","page":"API Reference","text":"synthesize_tactic(spec::FormalSpecification) -> String\n\nGenerates an automated, intelligent Lean 4 tactic script matching the specification shape:\n\nIf quantifiers are present: introduces variables (intros).\nIf branching (if / elseif) is present: splits conditionals (split_ifs) and solves branches.\nSolves goals via linarith, omega, decide, or rfl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.synthesize_tactic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.register_lean_type!","page":"API Reference","text":"register_lean_type!(jl_type, lean_type::LeanType)\nregister_lean_type!(jl_type, lean_name::String)\n\nRegisters a custom mapping from a Julia type (or Symbol) to a LeanType. Allows domain-specific packages to define their own Lean type bindings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.register_lean_type!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.julia_to_lean_type","page":"API Reference","text":"julia_to_lean_type(jl_type) -> LeanType\n\nMaps a Julia type or type expression to the corresponding LeanType.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.julia_to_lean_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.julia_to_lean_expr","page":"API Reference","text":"Recursively converts a Julia expression or value into a LeanExpr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.julia_to_lean_expr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.build_imperative_do_block","page":"API Reference","text":"build_imperative_do_block(stmts::Vector{Any}, fn_args::Vector{String}=String[], fn_arg_types::Dict{Symbol, Any}=Dict{Symbol, Any}()) -> LeanDoBlock\n\nTranslates an imperative sequence of Julia statements into a LeanDoBlock containing LeanDoStmts and an ending return value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.build_imperative_do_block"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.build_lean_block","page":"API Reference","text":"Translate a block of Julia statements into nested Lean let bindings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.build_lean_block"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.is_imperative_block","page":"API Reference","text":"is_imperative_block(stmts::Vector{Any}) -> Bool\n\nDetects whether a list of statements requires imperative Lean 4 do execution: contains loops (for, while), array mutations (a[i] = v, a[i] += v), in-place mutations (x += v), or variable reassignments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.is_imperative_block"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_call","page":"API Reference","text":"Translate Julia function calls and binary operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_call"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_comparison","page":"API Reference","text":"Translate chained comparison expressions (e.g. a <= b <= c -> a ≤ b ∧ b ≤ c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_lean_macro","page":"API Reference","text":"Translates supported macros (like @forall, @exists) into Lean quantifier expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_lean_macro"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_1based_index","page":"API Reference","text":"translate_1based_index(raw_idx, fn_args, loop_vars, fn_arg_types) -> LeanExpr\n\nTranslates a 1-based Julia index expression into a 0-based Lean 4 index:\n\nLoop variable i (already 0-based in Lean 4): i\nLoop variable offset i + k: i + k\nPositive integer / PNat p: p.1 - 1 (pure Nat arithmetic without any .toNat cast!)\nInteger literal k: k - 1 (statically converted at transpile-time)\nGeneral index expression x: (x - 1) (in pure Nat)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_1based_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.substitute_symbols","page":"API Reference","text":"substitute_symbols(ex, mapping::Dict{Symbol, Symbol})\n\nRecursively substitutes symbol identifiers in an expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.substitute_symbols"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.substitute_var","page":"API Reference","text":"substitute_var(e::LeanExpr, old_name::String, replacement::LeanExpr) -> LeanExpr\n\nReplaces occurrences of LeanVar(old_name) with replacement in e.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.substitute_var"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.is_pnat_type","page":"API Reference","text":"is_pnat_type(t) -> Bool\n\nChecks if a Julia or Lean type represents a strictly positive integer (ℕ+ / PNat).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.is_pnat_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.body_has_loops","page":"API Reference","text":"body_has_loops(body) -> Bool\n\nRecursively checks whether a function body contains imperative loops (while / for), which the plain SMT translation does not model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.body_has_loops"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.has_branching","page":"API Reference","text":"has_branching(ex) -> Bool\n\nChecks whether an AST contains conditional expressions (if, elseif).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.has_branching"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.has_non_linear","page":"API Reference","text":"Checks whether an expression AST contains non-linear multiplication or division between variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.has_non_linear"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.has_quantifier","page":"API Reference","text":"Checks whether an expression AST contains quantifier macro calls (@forall, @exists).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.has_quantifier"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.unwrap_ast_expr","page":"API Reference","text":"Unwraps single-statement blocks and returns, discarding LineNumberNodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.unwrap_ast_expr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.generate_smtlib_query","page":"API Reference","text":"generate_smtlib_query(spec::FormalSpecification; logic=\"\", produce_unsat_core::Bool=true) -> String\n\nGenerates a complete SMT-LIB 2.6 query file asserting the preconditions and the NEGATION of the postconditions. If satisfiable (sat), a counterexample exists. If unsatisfiable (unsat), extracts the minimal unsat core of preconditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.generate_smtlib_query"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.generate_bmc_query","page":"API Reference","text":"generate_bmc_query(spec::FormalSpecification, desc::LoopDescriptor; depth::Int=3, logic::String=\"ALL\") -> String\n\nGenerates an unrolled SMT-LIB 2.6 query encoding Bounded Model Checking up to depth iterations. Binds for loop variables to their unrolled iteration, encodes array updates as store, and falsifies not (invariants ∧ postconditions) at the final state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.generate_bmc_query"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.run_z3","page":"API Reference","text":"run_z3(smt_code::String; timeout::Float64=30.0) -> (status::Symbol, stdout_str::String, stderr_str::String, elapsed::Float64)\n\nExecutes Z3 on the given SMT-LIB code string. Returns :sat, :unsat, :unknown, or :error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.run_z3"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.parse_unsat_core","page":"API Reference","text":"parse_unsat_core(out::AbstractString) -> Vector{String}\n\nExtracts named assertion labels from a Z3 (get-unsat-core) response. Filters out internal definition and target names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.parse_unsat_core"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.parse_z3_model","page":"API Reference","text":"parse_z3_model(out::AbstractString) -> Dict{Symbol, Any}\n\nExtracts variable bindings from a Z3 (get-model) s-expression response.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.parse_z3_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.parse_smt_val","page":"API Reference","text":"parse_smt_val(s::AbstractString)\n\nParses an SMT-LIB scalar value representation (numbers, rationals, booleans) into Julia types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.parse_smt_val"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_smt_call","page":"API Reference","text":"Translate function calls and operators to SMT-LIB format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_smt_call"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_smt_comparison","page":"API Reference","text":"Translate chained comparisons (a <= b <= c) to conjunctions of SMT comparisons.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_smt_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.translate_smt_macro","page":"API Reference","text":"Translates supported macros (like @forall, @exists) into SMT-LIB quantifier expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.translate_smt_macro"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.build_smt_let_block","page":"API Reference","text":"Translate a block of let assignments into SMT let expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.build_smt_let_block"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.unwrap_smt_expr","page":"API Reference","text":"Unwraps single-statement blocks and returns, discarding LineNumberNodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.unwrap_smt_expr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.julia_to_smt_expr","page":"API Reference","text":"julia_to_smt_expr(ex) -> String\n\nRecursively translates a Julia expression or literal into an SMT-LIB 2.6 s-expression string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.julia_to_smt_expr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.julia_to_smt_type","page":"API Reference","text":"julia_to_smt_type(t) -> String\n\nMaps a Julia type or type symbol to its SMT-LIB sort (Real, Int, Bool, datatypes, or arrays).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.julia_to_smt_type"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LoopDescriptor","page":"API Reference","text":"LoopDescriptor\n\nDecomposed structure of a loop extracted from a function AST.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LoopDescriptor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.extract_loop_descriptor","page":"API Reference","text":"extract_loop_descriptor(spec::FormalSpecification) -> Union{Nothing, LoopDescriptor}\n\nInspects the body of a FormalSpecification and extracts loop structure, state initializers, updates (scalar and array), and invariant conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.extract_loop_descriptor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LeanConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"LeanConfig\n\nConfiguration for invoking the Lean 4 compiler and Lake build tool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LeanConfig"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.DEFAULT_LEAN_PROJECT_DIR","page":"API Reference","text":"Global override for target Lean 4 project directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.DEFAULT_LEAN_PROJECT_DIR"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.set_lean_project_dir!","page":"API Reference","text":"set_lean_project_dir!(dir::String)\n\nSets the global default Lean 4 project directory for Lake builds and verification runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.set_lean_project_dir!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.find_lean_project_dir","page":"API Reference","text":"find_lean_project_dir() -> String\n\nDiscovers an available Lean 4 / Lake project directory for imports and environment. Checks DEFAULT_LEAN_PROJECT_DIR, environment variables, current directory, and standard paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.find_lean_project_dir"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.find_lean_binary","page":"API Reference","text":"find_lean_binary() -> String\n\nLocates the lean compiler binary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.find_lean_binary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.find_lake_binary","page":"API Reference","text":"find_lake_binary() -> String\n\nLocates the lake build tool binary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.find_lake_binary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.run_lean","page":"API Reference","text":"run_lean(code::String; project_dir=find_lean_project_dir(),\n                       imports=[\"Mathlib.Tactic\"],\n                       set_options=[\"linter.unusedVariables false\", \"linter.unusedSectionVars false\", \"linter.unusedTactic false\", \"linter.unreachableTactic false\"],\n                       timeout=60.0) -> LeanRunResult\n\nRuns the Lean 4 compiler on the provided Lean code string and returns the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.run_lean"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JuLean.LeanRunResult","page":"API Reference","text":"Result of a Lean 4 verification run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.LeanRunResult"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.STRUCT_REGISTRY","page":"API Reference","text":"Global registry for formalized user structs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.STRUCT_REGISTRY"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#JuLean.CUSTOM_TYPE_REGISTRY","page":"API Reference","text":"Global registry for user-defined Julia-to-Lean type mappings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.CUSTOM_TYPE_REGISTRY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.export_lean_file","page":"API Reference","text":"export_lean_file(filepath::String;\n                 fns=collect(keys(SPEC_REGISTRY)),\n                 tactics=Dict{Symbol, String}(),\n                 imports=[\"Mathlib.Tactic\"],\n                 set_options=[\"linter.unusedVariables false\", \"linter.unusedTactic false\", \"linter.unreachableTactic false\", \"linter.unnecessarySeqFocus false\"]) -> String\n\nExports all registered structures and selected formalized functions into a standalone, compilable Lean 4 source file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.export_lean_file"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.verification_summary","page":"API Reference","text":"verification_summary() -> String\n\nGenerates a formatted summary table of all formalized specifications registered in JuLean, including argument counts, precondition/postcondition/invariant counts, and return types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.verification_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JuLean.simplify_expr","page":"API Reference","text":"simplify_expr(expr) -> Expr\n\nSimplifies an algebraic expression. When Metatheory.jl is loaded, the JuLeanMetatheoryExt extension performs equality saturation and term rewriting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JuLean.simplify_expr"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#AI-and-LLM-Agent-Integration-Guide","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"This guide is mirrored from the canonical root document AGENTS.md to provide explicit instructions for autonomous AI agents, coding assistants (Gemini, Claude, GPT, Cursor, Copilot), and automated code generators interacting with or integrating JuLean.jl.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"admonition is-info\">\n<header class=\"admonition-header\">Core Mandate</header>\n<div class=\"admonition-body\">\nAll AI agents integrating or modifying JuLean.jl must strictly uphold:\n<ul>\n  <li><b>English Only</b>: All comments, contracts, documentation, and commit messages.</li>\n  <li><b>Option A Architecture</b>: Zero runtime dependency on JuLean in client core libraries (package extension via <code>[weakdeps]</code>).</li>\n  <li><b>Exact Arithmetic</b>: $\\mathbb{Q}$ / <code>Rational{BigInt}</code>, avoiding floating-point rounding traps.</li>\n  <li><b>Absolute Soundness</b>: ZERO tolerance for <code>sorry</code> in Lean 4 proofs.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n","title":"AI & LLM Agent Integration Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#1.-Executive-Summary-and-Core-Philosophy","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"JuLean.jl unifies Design-by-Contract (DbC), Automated SMT Falsification (Z3), and Interactive Mechanized Proof (Lean 4 / Mathlib) for Julia scientific and numerical code.\n\nThe guiding axiom of JuLean is:\n\n\"In numerical optimization and high-stakes scientific computing, speed without mathematical correctness is just faster bugs.\"\n\nWhen acting as an AI assistant to integrate JuLean into a package (such as Sirocco.jl, Kaos.jl, or any scientific solver), you must guarantee two non-negotiable properties:\n\nZero Runtime Dependency Overhead: Production users must never pay a runtime, compilation, or dependency penalty.\nAbsolute Mathematical Soundness: Proofs must be genuine, mechanized theorems without placeholders (sorry).\n\n","title":"1. Executive Summary & Core Philosophy"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#2.-Canonical-Integration-Architecture:-\"Option-A\"-(The-Sirocco-Pattern)","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Never add JuLean.jl or z3_jll as direct dependencies in a solver's [deps] section. Instead, implement the Option A package extension pattern established in Sirocco.jl:\n\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                   Tier 1: Core Solver (e.g. Sirocco.jl)                │\n│  - src/simplex/pricing.jl, ratio_test.jl, solver.jl                    │\n│  - 100% pure Julia, high-performance, generic typing                   │\n│  - Contracts written in Markdown docstrings (ZERO JuLean dependency)   │\n└────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘\n                                     │\n                  Optional Dynamic Extension Trigger:\n                       `using JuLean, MyPackage`\n                                     │\n                                     ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│              Tier 2: Package Extension (ext/MyPackageJuLeanExt.jl)     │\n│  - Registered via [weakdeps] in Project.toml                           │\n│  - JuLean.formalize!(...) via docstring reflection                     │\n│  - JuLean.register_lean_type!(...) for custom types / enums            │\n│  - JuLean.register_runtime_only!(...) to strip debug checks            │\n│  - Two-level verification runners (:Z3 and :Lean4)                     │\n└────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘\n                                     │\n                                     ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│              Tier 3: Formal Verification Backends                      │\n│  - Level 1: Z3 SMT solver (bundled via z3_jll, push-button falsification)│\n│  - Level 2: Lean 4 + Mathlib (interactive / batch mechanized proofs)   │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘","title":"2. Canonical Integration Architecture: \"Option A\" (The Sirocco Pattern)"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#2.1-Project.toml-Configuration","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"In the client package's Project.toml:\n\n[weakdeps]\nJuLean = \"60d1f2a1-a197-4d78-8ba3-1a6337fe4745\"\n\n[extensions]\nMyPackageJuLeanExt = \"JuLean\"\n\nFor test environments, add JuLean to [extras] and include it in test targets.\n\n","title":"2.1 Project.toml Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#3.-Contract-Authoring:-Docstring-Reflection","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"To keep the core solver free of macro dependencies, write contracts directly in Markdown docstrings. JuLean's reflection engine automatically parses docstrings at extension initialization time.","title":"3. Contract Authoring: Docstring Reflection"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#3.1-Docstring-Syntax-Standards","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"\"\"\"\n    reduced_cost(cost, pi_u, pi_v, gamma)\n\nCompute the generalized reduced cost of an arc.\n\n- `@requires gamma > 0`\n- `@ensures result == cost - pi_u + gamma * pi_v`\n\"\"\"\nfunction reduced_cost(cost, pi_u, pi_v, gamma)\n    return cost - pi_u + gamma * pi_v\nend","title":"3.1 Docstring Syntax Standards"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#3.2-Formal-Keywords-and-Conventions","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Keyword / Symbol Meaning Example\n- \\@requires <expr>`` Function precondition - \\@requires lb <= ub && tol >= 0``\n- \\@ensures <expr>`` Function postcondition - \\@ensures result >= lb && result <= ub``\n- \\@invariant <expr>`` Loop or state invariant - \\@invariant s >= 0 && i >= 0``\nresult The returned value - \\@ensures result == x + y``\n⟹, -->, implies(p, q) Logical implication - \\@ensures (dir == 1 ⟹ result <= ub)``\n@forall(binding, cond) Universal quantifier - \\@ensures @forall(e in 1:m, flow[e] >= 0)``\n@exists(binding, cond) Existential quantifier - \\@ensures @exists(v in 1:n, pi[v] == 0)``","title":"3.2 Formal Keywords & Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#3.3-Golden-Rules-for-Contracts","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Always use result in @ensures to reference the return value of the function.\nDo not write tautologies: E.g., @requires x > 0 followed by @ensures x > 0 tests nothing about the function.\nAvoid floating-point epsilons in contracts: Prefer algebraic inequalities (tol >= 0, delta == 0) rather than abs(x) < 1e-9.\n\n","title":"3.3 Golden Rules for Contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#4.-Stripping-Runtime-Checks:-register_runtime_only!","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"In production solvers, kernels often include debug assertions or runtime integrity checks:\n\nfunction ratio_step(f, bound, dir)\n    if _contracts_enabled()\n        _check_ratio_bounds(f, bound, dir)\n    end\n    return abs(bound - f)\nend\n\nIf transpiled directly, SMT and Lean 4 engines will fail trying to parse imperative error-throwing logic.","title":"4. Stripping Runtime Checks: register_runtime_only!"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#4.1-How-to-Strip-Runtime-Checks-Cleanly","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"In the extension's __init__(), register all debug guards and checker functions as runtime-only:\n\nfunction __init__()\n    # Register debug guards and assertion helpers\n    JuLean.register_runtime_only!(MyPackage._contracts_enabled)\n    JuLean.register_runtime_only!(MyPackage._check_ratio_bounds)\n\n    # Formalize kernels after registering runtime-only helpers\n    JuLean.formalize!(MyPackage.ratio_step)\nend\n\nJuLean Mechanism: JuLean's AST preprocessor recursively strips any statement containing a call to a registered runtime-only function. The production kernel retains its debug checks for testing, while the formal pipeline sees only the pure mathematical computation.\n\nNote on Loops: JuLean deliberately preserves while and for loops even if their body contains runtime-only calls; only the runtime statements inside the loop are removed.","title":"4.1 How to Strip Runtime Checks Cleanly"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#4.2-Performance-Pragmas-Unwrapping","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"JuLean automatically unwraps macro pragmas:\n\n@inbounds, @simd, @inline, @noinline, @fastmath, @propagate_inbounds\n\nYou can use these performance annotations freely in production kernels. JuLean's AST parser unrolls them so they do not pollute the formal SMT or Lean 4 translation.\n\n","title":"4.2 Performance Pragmas Unwrapping"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#5.-Exact-Arithmetic-(\\mathbb{Q})-vs-Floating-Point-Fallacies","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"When building formal models or certificates:\n\nLean 4 and SMT operate over exact rationals (mathbbQ / LeanRat).\nIn Julia, test certificates and formal models must use Rational{BigInt} or exact integers (Int64).\nNever introduce Float64 round-off tricks into formal contracts. If an algorithm requires a numerical tolerance, make tol an explicit parameter with @requires tol >= 0.\n\n","title":"5. Exact Arithmetic (mathbbQ) vs Floating-Point Fallacies"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#6.-Architecture-Partitioning:-Micro-Kernels-vs-Imperative-Driver-Loops","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"A common failure mode for AI agents is trying to transpile an entire 500-line imperative while-loop solver into a single Lean 4 function. Do not do this.\n\nDivide your solver into two distinct tiers:","title":"6. Architecture Partitioning: Micro-Kernels vs Imperative Driver Loops"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#Tier-A:-Algorithmic-Micro-Kernels-(Transpiled-AST)","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Examples: reduced_cost, is_arc_eligible, ratio_step, update_flow, check_complementary_slackness.\nCharacteristics: Pure functions or localized in-place mutations.\nVerification: Transpiled automatically by JuLean into Lean 4 def + theorem declarations and verified via Z3 or automated tactics (linarith, omega, split_ifs).","title":"Tier A: Algorithmic Micro-Kernels (Transpiled AST)"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#Tier-B:-Imperative-Driver-Loops-(Inductive-Step-Bridge)","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Examples: solve_simplex!, solve_dual_gmcf.\nCharacteristics: Outer while loop, mutable graph basis, early terminations, convergence tolerances.\nThe Step-Invariant Pattern:\nDefine an abstracted state structure in Lean 4 (structure DriverState).\nDefine the inductive loop invariant (def DriverInvariant : Prop).\nFormulate the single-step preservation theorem (theorem step_preserves_invariant).\nBridge to an existing formal library (such as DunesLean or Mathlib).\nReport the status honestly in Julia:\n:Z3 backend reports StatusUnverified (loops over dynamic structures are outside quantifier-free SMT scope).\n:Lean4 backend reports StatusVerified when the step bridge elaborates with zero sorry.\n\n","title":"Tier B: Imperative Driver Loops (Inductive Step Bridge)"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#7.-Custom-Types,-Inlining,-and-Proof-Tactics","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"","title":"7. Custom Types, Inlining, and Proof Tactics"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#7.1-Registering-Custom-Types-and-Enums","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"If your Julia kernel uses an enum or struct:\n\nJuLean.register_lean_type!(MyPackage.ArcStatus, \"ArcStatus\")","title":"7.1 Registering Custom Types & Enums"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#7.2-Inlining-Helper-Functions","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"If a kernel calls a small mathematical helper, register it for automatic inlining:\n\nJuLean.register_inline!(:abs_diff, [:a, :b], :(a >= b ? a - b : b - a))","title":"7.2 Inlining Helper Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#7.3-Supplying-Lean-4-Proof-Tactics","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"JuLean automatically synthesizes default tactics (linarith, omega, split_ifs). For non-trivial theorems, define a KERNEL_TACTICS dictionary in your extension:\n\nconst KERNEL_TACTICS = Dict{Symbol, String}(\n    :reduced_cost =>\n        \"\"\"\n        by\n          unfold reduced_cost\n          ring\n        \"\"\",\n\n    :ratio_step =>\n        \"\"\"\n        by\n          unfold ratio_step\n          by_cases h : forward <;> simp [h] <;> rcases h_pre_1 with ⟨h1, h2, h3⟩ <;> linarith\n        \"\"\"\n)","title":"7.3 Supplying Lean 4 Proof Tactics"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#7.4-The-Strict-Soundness-Rule:-ZERO-sorry","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"JuLean.jl explicitly inspects Lean compiler warnings and output. Any proof that emits sorry is rejected with StatusError. Never submit or generate sorry placeholders.\n\n","title":"7.4 The Strict Soundness Rule: ZERO sorry"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#8.-Implementing-the-Extension:-Complete-Template","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Below is the idiomatic structure for ext/MyPackageJuLeanExt.jl:\n\nmodule MyPackageJuLeanExt\n\nusing JuLean\nusing MyPackage\n\nconst KERNEL_FUNCTIONS = [\n    MyPackage.reduced_cost,\n    MyPackage.ratio_step,\n    MyPackage.update_flow,\n]\n\nconst KERNEL_NAMES = [nameof(f) for f in KERNEL_FUNCTIONS]\n\nconst KERNEL_TACTICS = Dict{Symbol, String}(\n    :reduced_cost => \"by unfold reduced_cost; ring\",\n    :ratio_step   => \"by unfold ratio_step; split_ifs <;> linarith\",\n)\n\nfunction __init__()\n    # 1. Register types & enums\n    JuLean.register_lean_type!(MyPackage.ArcStatus, \"ArcStatus\")\n\n    # 2. Register runtime-only checks before formalizing\n    JuLean.register_runtime_only!(MyPackage._contracts_enabled)\n    JuLean.register_runtime_only!(MyPackage._check_bounds!)\n\n    # 3. Formalize kernels from docstrings\n    for f in KERNEL_FUNCTIONS\n        JuLean.formalize!(f)\n    end\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    MyPackage.verify_formal_specifications(; backend=:Z3, timeout=30.0)\n\"\"\"\nfunction MyPackage.verify_formal_specifications(; backend::Symbol=:Z3, timeout::Float64=30.0)\n    results = Dict{Symbol, JuLean.VerificationResult}()\n    for s in KERNEL_NAMES\n        tac = get(KERNEL_TACTICS, s, \"\")\n        results[s] = JuLean.verify(s; backend=backend, timeout=timeout, tactic=tac)\n    end\n    return results\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    MyPackage.export_lean_specifications(filepath::String)\n\"\"\"\nfunction MyPackage.export_lean_specifications(filepath::String)\n    return JuLean.export_lean_file(filepath; fns=KERNEL_NAMES, tactics=KERNEL_TACTICS)\nend\n\nend # module MyPackageJuLeanExt\n\n","title":"8. Implementing the Extension: Complete Template"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#9.-Verification-Testing-Pattern-(test/test_formal_verification.jl)","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Always provide comprehensive verification tests in the client package:\n\nusing Test\nusing JuLean\nusing MyPackage\n\n@testset \"JuLean Formal Verification\" begin\n    # 1. Verify extension loading and registry population\n    @test isdefined(MyPackage, :verify_formal_specifications)\n    for s in [:reduced_cost, :ratio_step]\n        @test haskey(JuLean.SPEC_REGISTRY, s)\n        spec = JuLean.SPEC_REGISTRY[s]\n        @test !isempty(spec.postconditions)\n        @test any(p -> occursin(\"result\", string(p)), spec.postconditions)\n    end\n\n    # 2. Level 1: Z3 SMT Verification Sweep\n    results_z3 = MyPackage.verify_formal_specifications(backend=:Z3)\n    for (name, res) in results_z3\n        @test JuLean.is_verified(res)\n    end\n\n    # 3. Level 2: Lean 4 Mechanized Proof Sweep (if Lean 4 installed)\n    if JuLean.find_lean_project_dir() !== nothing\n        results_lean = MyPackage.verify_formal_specifications(backend=:Lean4)\n        for (name, res) in results_lean\n            @test res.status == JuLean.StatusVerified\n        end\n    end\nend\n\n","title":"9. Verification Testing Pattern (test/test_formal_verification.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#10.-AI-/-LLM-Quick-Checklist:-Do's-and-Don'ts","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"","title":"10. AI / LLM Quick Checklist: Do's and Don'ts"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#DO:","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Adhere to Option A: Keep the client package's src/ 100% free of direct using JuLean statements.\nUse docstring contracts (-@requires ... - ensures ) in client code.\nReference return values as result in @ensures postconditions.\nRegister all debug checks with JuLean.register_runtime_only! before formalization.\nDecouple driver loops from micro-kernels: Transpile micro-kernels; bridge drivers via step-invariants.\nUse exact rational arithmetic (mathbbQ) in certificates and formal specifications.\nEnsure zero sorry in all Lean 4 tactics and bridge files.","title":"✅ DO:"},{"category":"section","location":"agents/#NEVER:","page":"AI & LLM Guide","text":"Never add JuLean to [deps] of a production library.\nNever hardcode floating-point epsilons (1e-9, 1e-12) into mathematical contracts.\nNever attempt to transpile full iterative state machines directly with extract_formal_spec.\nNever accept or generate sorry in Lean proofs.\nNever write tautological postconditions that do not assert meaningful properties of result.\nNever leave runtime assertions active during transpilation without registering them with register_runtime_only!.","title":"❌ NEVER:"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Verification-Case-Studies-and-Emblematic-Examples","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"This chapter presents five emblematic case studies illustrating how JuLean.jl applies formal verification, SMT falsification, bounded model checking, and interactive theorem proving to real-world computational problems.\n\nEvery example in this guide is fully specified, executable, and soundly verified:\n\nPreconditions (@requires) and postconditions (@ensures) completely characterize functional correctness.\nNo incomplete proofs, missing postconditions, or sorry shortcuts are used.\nEvery verified routine produces StatusVerified.\n\n","title":"Verification Case Studies & Emblematic Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#The-Verification-Soundness-Taxonomy","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"JuLean adheres to a soundness-first principle: a method is never falsely reported as verified if its specification is incomplete. The verification engine distinguishes five precise statuses:\n\nStatus Meaning When It Occurs\nStatusVerified Fully Verified The Lean 4 kernel proved the theorem without sorry, or Z3 proved the query unsatisfiable across all valid inputs.\nStatusCounterexample Refuted / Falsified Z3 found a concrete counterexample demonstrating that the specification is violated.\nStatusSyntaxChecked Type-Checked Only A function definition is syntactically well-formed in Lean 4, but no postconditions (@ensures) were declared, so nothing was mathematically proven.\nStatusUnverified Not Statically Proved A specification contains loops that pure SMT cannot prove without unrolling (use backend=:BMC), or was not submitted to a proof backend.\nStatusError Proof Failure The backend timed out, encountered a syntax error, or attempted to use the sorry axiom (which JuLean strictly rejects).\n\n","title":"The Verification Soundness Taxonomy"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#1.-The-Canonical-Binary-Search-Midpoint-(Overflow-and-Bound-Falsification)","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"The binary search midpoint calculation is one of the most famous specification bugs in computer science: Joshua Bloch identified in 2006 that the implementation mid = (low + high) / 2 used in textbooks and the Java Standard Library for twenty years was susceptible to integer overflow when low + high > typemax(Int).\n\nWith JuLean, we specify exact interval boundaries and verify whether the midpoint remains strictly enclosed.","title":"1. The Canonical Binary Search Midpoint (Overflow & Bound Falsification)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Falsification-with-SMT-/-Z3","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"Consider an implementation that forgets to ensure the upper bound or suffers from an off-by-one boundary bug:\n\nusing JuLean\n\n@formalize function buggy_midpoint(low::Real, high::Real)::Real\n    @requires low <= high\n    @ensures low <= result <= high\n    # Bug: adds an extra offset that can exceed `high`\n    return low + (high - low) + 1\nend\n\nres = verify(:buggy_midpoint; backend=:Z3)\nprintln(\"Status: \", res.status)\n# => StatusCounterexample\nprintln(\"Counterexample: \", res.counterexample)\n# => Dict(:low => 0//1, :high => 0//1)\n\nZ3 immediately isolates an exact rational counterexample showing that when low = high = 0, the returned value 1 violates result <= high.","title":"Falsification with SMT / Z3"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Certified-Correct-Implementation","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"We now implement the canonical midpoint calculation and verify it across both Z3 and the Lean 4 kernel:\n\n@formalize function safe_midpoint(low::Real, high::Real)::Real\n    @requires low <= high\n    @ensures low <= result <= high\n    return low + (high - low) / 2\nend\n\n# 1. Instant automated SMT check (~10 ms)\nres_z3 = verify(:safe_midpoint; backend=:Z3)\n@assert res_z3.status == StatusVerified\n\n# 2. Mechanized kernel proof in Lean 4 + Mathlib (ℚ arithmetic)\nres_lean = verify(:safe_midpoint; backend=:Lean4)\n@assert res_lean.status == StatusVerified\nprintln(\"Lean 4 Proof accepted without sorry!\")\n\nThe Lean 4 backend automatically transpiles safe_midpoint to exact rational arithmetic (mathbbQ), constructs the theorem safe_midpoint_spec, and discharges the goal via automated linear arithmetic tactics (constructor <;> linarith).\n\n","title":"Certified Correct Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#2.-Operations-Research:-Arc-Reduced-Costs-and-Simplex-Pivot-Pricing","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"In network optimization algorithms (such as the Dual Network Simplex in Sirocco.jl and DuNeS), the network simplex basis status depends entirely on the signs of arc reduced costs:\n\nc_uv^pi = c_uv - (pi_u - pi_v)\n\nA single incorrect sign or boundary calculation can cause cyclic basis pivoting or infeasible flow assignments.","title":"2. Operations Research: Arc Reduced Costs & Simplex Pivot Pricing"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Struct-Modeling-and-Functional-Invariants","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"JuLean allows composite domain structures to be registered directly with @formalize struct:\n\n@formalize struct Arc\n    cost::Real\n    lower::Real\n    upper::Real\nend\n\n@formalize function reduced_cost(arc::Arc, pi_u::Real, pi_v::Real)::Real\n    @requires arc.lower <= arc.upper\n    @ensures result == arc.cost - (pi_u - pi_v)\n    return arc.cost - (pi_u - pi_v)\nend\n\nres_rc_z3 = verify(:reduced_cost; backend=:Z3)\n@assert res_rc_z3.status == StatusVerified\n\nres_rc_lean = verify(:reduced_cost; backend=:Lean4)\n@assert res_rc_lean.status == StatusVerified","title":"Struct Modeling and Functional Invariants"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Pivot-Pricing-Eligibility-and-Unsat-Cores","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"An arc at its lower bound is eligible to enter the primal basis if its reduced cost is strictly negative:\n\n@formalize function is_primal_eligible(rc::Real, flow::Real, lower::Real)::Bool\n    @requires flow == lower\n    @ensures result ⟹ (rc < 0)\n    return rc < 0\nend\n\nres_elig = verify(:is_primal_eligible; backend=:Z3)\n@assert res_elig.status == StatusVerified\n\n# Lean 4 proof verification\nres_elig_lean = verify(:is_primal_eligible; backend=:Lean4)\n@assert res_elig_lean.status == StatusVerified\n\n","title":"Pivot Pricing Eligibility & Unsat Cores"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#3.-Universal-Quantifiers:-Characterizing-the-Greatest-Lower-Bound-(\\forall,-\\exists)","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"Proving that a function returns min(x, y) requires more than checking result <= x and result <= y. A complete functional specification must prove that the result is the greatest lower bound (infimum):\n\nforall t in mathbbR (t le x land t le y) implies t le textresult\n\nJuLean supports first-order quantifiers @forall and @exists alongside Unicode implication ⟹.\n\n@formalize function safe_min(x::Real, y::Real)::Real\n    @requires x >= 0 && y >= 0\n    # 1. Lower bound properties\n    @ensures result <= x\n    @ensures result <= y\n    # 2. Greatest lower bound property (universal quantifier)\n    @ensures @forall(t::Real, (t <= x && t <= y) ⟹ t <= result)\n    return x < y ? x : y\nend\n\n# Verification with Z3 (quantified SMT theory)\nres_z3 = verify(:safe_min; backend=:Z3)\n@assert res_z3.status == StatusVerified\n\n# Deductive proof in Lean 4\nlean_tactic = \"\"\"\nby\n  unfold safe_min\n  refine ⟨by split_ifs <;> linarith, by split_ifs <;> linarith, ?_⟩\n  intro t ht\n  split_ifs <;> linarith\n\"\"\"\n\nres_lean = verify(:safe_min; backend=:Lean4, tactic=lean_tactic)\n@assert res_lean.status == StatusVerified\n\nIn Lean 4, the transpiled theorem statement mirrors standard textbook mathematics:\n\ntheorem safe_min_spec (x : ℚ) (y : ℚ) (h_pre_1 : x ≥ 0 ∧ y ≥ 0) :\n  safe_min x y ≤ x ∧ safe_min x y ≤ y ∧ (∀ (t : ℚ), t ≤ x ∧ t ≤ y → t ≤ (safe_min x y))\n\n","title":"3. Universal Quantifiers: Characterizing the Greatest Lower Bound (forall exists)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#4.-Collections-and-Safe-Array-Indexing-(1-Based-Julia-Semantics)","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"In Julia, collections are 1-indexed by convention (1 <= i <= length(arr)), unlike 0-indexed languages such as C, Python, or raw Lean 4.\n\nJuLean provides first-class support for 1-based indexing via JuLean.PosInt (positive integers 1, 2, ..., transpiled to Mathlib ℕ+). The transpiler automatically maps Julia's 1-based array accesses arr[i] into Lean 4's 0-based array projections arr[(i.1 - 1)]!, while maintaining exact bounds semantics across both Z3 and Lean 4.\n\n@formalize function safe_lookup(arr::Vector{Real}, i::JuLean.PosInt)::Real\n    @requires 1 <= i <= length(arr)\n    @ensures result == arr[i]\n    return arr[i]\nend\n\n# 1. Verified by Z3 SMT array theory\nres_arr_z3 = verify(:safe_lookup; backend=:Z3)\n@assert res_arr_z3.status == StatusVerified\n\n# 2. Verified by Lean 4 Array projection (automatically proved via rfl)\nres_arr_lean = verify(:safe_lookup; backend=:Lean4)\n@assert res_arr_lean.status == StatusVerified\n\nThe resulting Lean 4 definition and theorem cleanly bridge Julia's 1-based indexing to Lean 4:\n\ndef safe_lookup (arr : Array (ℚ)) (i : ℕ+) : ℚ :=\n  arr[(i.1 - 1)]!\n\ntheorem safe_lookup_spec (arr : Array (ℚ)) (i : ℕ+) (h_pre_1 : 1 ≤ i ∧ i ≤ arr.size) :\n  safe_lookup arr i = arr[(i.1 - 1)]! := by\n  unfold safe_lookup\n  rfl","title":"4. Collections and Safe Array Indexing (1-Based Julia Semantics)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Refuting-Insecure-Lookups","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"If a developer accidentally returns the first element arr[1] regardless of i:\n\n@formalize function buggy_lookup(arr::Vector{Real}, i::JuLean.PosInt)::Real\n    @requires 1 <= i <= length(arr)\n    @ensures result == arr[i]\n    return arr[1] # Bug: returns the first element regardless of i!\nend\n\nres_bug = verify(:buggy_lookup; backend=:Z3)\n@assert res_bug.status == StatusCounterexample\n\nZ3 synthesizes an array model where arr[1] ≠ arr[i] (for example, with length(arr) = 2 and i = 2), proving the implementation incorrect in milliseconds.\n\n","title":"Refuting Insecure Lookups"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#5.-Inductive-Loop-Invariants-and-Bounded-Model-Checking-(BMC)","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"Iterative algorithms pose a classical challenge for automated verification: unconstrained loops cannot be easily translated into static, non-recursive SMT formulas.\n\nJuLean resolves this through two complementary mechanisms:\n\nRuntime & Static @invariant annotations: Asserted on every iteration during execution.\nBounded Model Checking (backend=:BMC): Symbolically unrolls loops up to depth k to verify invariant preservation and detect multi-step state violations.\n\n@formalize function loop_sum(n::Int)::Int\n    @requires n >= 0\n    @ensures result >= 0\n    s = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < n\n        @invariant s >= 0 && i >= 0\n        s = s + i\n        i = i + 1\n    end\n    return s\nend\n\n# Verify invariant preservation up to depth k = 10\nres_bmc = verify(:loop_sum; backend=:BMC, depth=10)\n@assert res_bmc.status == StatusVerified\nprintln(\"Loop verified up to 10 iterations without invariant violation.\")","title":"5. Inductive Loop Invariants & Bounded Model Checking (BMC)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Intercepting-Iterative-Bugs-via-BMC","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"If an off-by-one or sign error occurs in the loop body:\n\n@formalize function faulty_loop(n::Int)::Int\n    @requires n >= 0\n    @ensures result >= 0\n    s = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < n\n        @invariant s >= 0\n        s = s - 1 # Breaks invariant!\n        i = i + 1\n    end\n    return s\nend\n\nres_faulty = verify(:faulty_loop; backend=:BMC, depth=2)\n@assert res_faulty.status == StatusCounterexample\n\nprintln(\"Counterexample captured at step 1: \", res_faulty.counterexample[:s_1])\n# => -1\n\nBMC detects the invariant violation at iteration 1 and extracts the exact symbolic state (s_1 = -1), pin-pointing the step where correctness collapsed.\n\n","title":"Intercepting Iterative Bugs via BMC"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#6.-Standalone-Lean-4-Export","page":"Case Studies & Examples","text":"All verified specifications and theorems can be exported into a standalone, compilable Lean 4 source file for external auditing or formal publication:\n\nJuLean.export_lean_file(\"build/VerifiedSolvers.lean\"; fns=[:safe_midpoint, :reduced_cost, :safe_min])\n\nThe exported file contains self-contained Mathlib imports, structure declarations, definitions, and theorems, fully compilable by lake build.","title":"6. Standalone Lean 4 Export"},{"category":"section","location":"#JuLean.jl","page":"Home","text":"<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"assets/logo.svg\" alt=\"JuLean.jl Logo\" width=\"560\">\n</p>\n\nA Formal Verification and Design-by-Contract Bridge between Julia and Lean 4.\n\n","title":"JuLean.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"JuLean.jl bridges the high-performance scientific computing capabilities of Julia with the mechanized interactive theorem proving powers of Lean 4, supplemented by SMT / Z3 for automated counterexample search and E-graph equality saturation for algebraic simplification.\n\nJuLean is designed to be 100% domain-agnostic: it can verify algorithms ranging from numerical optimization (such as Sirocco.jl and DuNeS) to abstract graph data structures and pure arithmetic routines.\n\n","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Multi-Tiered-Verification-Pipeline","page":"Home","text":"JuLean implements a tiered verification architecture:\n\nLevel 0 — Dynamic Design-by-Contract (DbC): Zero-overhead runtime contract enforcement (@contract, @requires, @ensures, @invariant). Contracts can be written directly in Julia code via macros or non-invasively inside Markdown docstrings (parsed automatically via docstring reflection, keeping production code free of JuLean dependencies). Contracts can be toggled on/off globally with enable_contracts! / disable_contracts!.\nLevel 1 — Rapid SMT Falsification (Z3): Automated verification and counterexample search via native SMT-LIB 2.6 generation and z3_jll. Detects failing contracts and decodes exact rational counterexamples (Rational{BigInt}) in milliseconds.\nLevel 2 — Bounded Model Checking (BMC): Symbolic loop unrolling up to depth k (JuLean.verify(fn; backend=:BMC, depth=k)) to verify invariant preservation and trace multi-step state violations.\nLevel 3 — Mechanized Interactive Proofs (Lean 4): Full deductive formal verification targeting Lean 4, Lake, and Mathlib. Generates typed definitions (def), specification conjectures (theorem ..._spec), and automatically synthesizes proof scripts using tactics (linarith, omega, split_ifs, rfl, decide).\n\n","title":"Multi-Tiered Verification Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Approach-1:-Non-Invasive-Contracts-in-Markdown-Docstrings","page":"Home","text":"Ideal for production packages that cannot take a dependency on JuLean. Contracts are specified directly in the standard docstring:\n\n\"\"\"\n    reduced_cost(cost, pi_u, pi_v)\n\n- `@requires cost >= 0`\n- `@ensures result == cost - (pi_u - pi_v)`\n\"\"\"\nreduced_cost(cost, pi_u, pi_v) = cost - (pi_u - pi_v)\n\nusing JuLean\n\n# Reflect AST and parse contracts from Markdown docstring:\nJuLean.formalize!(reduced_cost)\n\n# Verify with Z3:\nres = JuLean.verify(:reduced_cost; backend=:Z3)\nprintln(res.status) # StatusVerified","title":"Approach 1: Non-Invasive Contracts in Markdown Docstrings"},{"category":"section","location":"#Approach-2:-Direct-In-Body-Contracts-with-@formalize","page":"Home","text":"Ideal for formal verification modules, self-contained algorithms, and rapid prototyping:\n\nusing JuLean\n\n# 1. Define a function with formal contracts\n@formalize function clamp_flow(f::Real, lb::Real, ub::Real)::Real\n    @requires lb <= ub\n    @ensures lb <= result <= ub\n    if f < lb\n        return lb\n    elseif f > ub\n        return ub\n    else\n        return f\n    end\nend\n\n# 2. Automated SMT / Z3 verification\nres_z3 = JuLean.verify(:clamp_flow; backend=:Z3)\nprintln(res_z3.status) # StatusVerified\n\n# 3. Mechanized Lean 4 proof\nres_lean = JuLean.verify(:clamp_flow; backend=:Lean4)\nprintln(res_lean.status) # StatusVerified\n\n# 4. Standalone Lean 4 file export\nJuLean.export_lean_file(\"FormalModel.lean\")","title":"Approach 2: Direct In-Body Contracts with @formalize"}]
}
